WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.720
Astronomers have discovered the youngest known exoplanet, a baby world still forming at just 1 million years old. This is fascinating frontiers, episode 197. Elias 224b is a 1 million year old world still embedded in its birth disc. The planet sits roughly 450 light years away. Observations captured material actively accruing onto the forming body. The detection provides a direct view of a planet in its earliest growth phase.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:34.900
This snapshot challenges current models of when planets begin clearing gaps in their

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:35.320
disks.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.420
From watching planets assemble to understanding how the elements inside them are forged,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.800
we turned to new nuclear data from stellar interiors.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.440
An international research team reported the first experimental investigation of how Krypton

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.200
88 absorbs neutrons.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.360
The work was published in communications physics.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.600
The measurements help close gaps in models of slow neutron capture element formation.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.580
These data explain abundance patterns seen in ancient stars and future planets.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.560
Imagine holding a single teaspoon of material from the heart of a red giant star.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:15.380
That tiny volume would contain more neutrons passing through it in one second than all the

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.300
atoms in the entire Pacific Ocean.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.480
Inside these stars, the slow neutron capture process steadily builds heavier elements by adding

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:29.260
one neutron at a time to existing nuclei. Each addition is followed by a beta decay that

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:35.260
turns a neutron into a proton. The recent crypton 88 measurements show exactly how often

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:40.880
a nucleus absorbs a neutron before decaying. That rate controls how much strontium ends up

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.880
in the star's outer layers and later in the planets that form around it. The process runs

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:50.900
at temperatures around 100 million Kelvin and densities where a single nucleus might

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:57.120
weight centuries between captures. Over millions of years, the chain climbs from iron all the way past

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.420
strontium. We can measure the resulting abundance patterns in ancient stars,

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.860
but globally yet knows why the observed strontium levels sometimes exceed with the best nuclear

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:12.620
data predict. While nuclear reactions quietly build the elements, Europe is building something more

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:18.580
tangible in orbit. Nearly 80 companies have joined the European Space Agency's secure connectivity

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:24.160
activity effort. The agency announced the expansion of its iris-squared program. This

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.860
new phase examines mission concepts and services in low-earth orbit. The initiative aims

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:35.320
to strengthen Europe's space-based secure communications. Europe's commercial push contrast

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.700
with a quieter national launch from Russia. Russia appears to have launched a Soyuz from

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:47.960
Placetsk at around 1650 UTC on September 17. The launch probably carried another GLONASS

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:54.080
navigation satellite. No official confirmation has been released yet. Orbital launch tracker Jonathan

00:02:54.080 --> 00:03:00.300
McDowell reported the event. This would add to Russia's navigation constellation. From navigation

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.880
satellites to research hardware heading for the space station, NASA will carry new experiments in

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:11.920
medicine and biology, along with solar arrays for the space station. The agency shared details ahead

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.580
of an upcoming launch. These experiments will support ongoing research aboard the orbiting laboratory.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.500
Solar arrays will help maintain power systems on the station.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.060
While agencies prep missions, smaller nations are also reaching orbit.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.640
The Technical University of Moldova is preparing to launch a new satellite.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.060
The project builds on prior nano-satellite achievements by the university.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.040
This next spacecraft will continue Moldova's contributions to space research.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.660
Launch preparations are now underway.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.900
From building satellites to watching weather on worlds we cannot reach,

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.260
Scientists from Trinity College developed a powerful way to unravel changing weather patterns

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.280
on distant worlds.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:59.400
The approach was tested on the well-studied brown dwarf S-I-M-P-0136.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.560
Weather on this object is largely shaped by changes in temperature and the vertical structure

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.280
of its clouds.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.100
The method separates temperature effects from cloud dynamics.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.440
Understanding distant weather leads us back to the chemistry that built our own solar system.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:20.220
researchers at the National High Magnetic Field Laboratory and Brookhaven National Laboratory

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.900
teamed up to examine the complex makeup of meteorites.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.940
The work provides a fresh look at molecules preserved inside these ancient rocks.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.580
The study offers new glimpses into our solar system's past chemistry.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.140
Ancient chemistry preserved in meteorites finds a parallel in hidden chemistry on Mars.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:46.220
Mars. Carbonate minerals form when water-carrying dissolved carbon dioxide reacts with rock. Mars has

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:51.780
surprisingly little carbonate on its surface compared with climate model predictions. The study

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:57.960
examines what ancient Mars rocks reveal about hidden carbonate stores. Composition of these minerals

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:04.680
reflects past water and atmospheric chemistry. From planetary surfaces to a star that refused to

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:10.360
fade quietly. Thirty years ago, an amateur astronomer noticed a white dwarf star suddenly

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:16.020
getting brighter. New research shows this is due to a very late thermal pulse, a type

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:21.200
of helium flash. The star has heated up and become brighter and is now called a born

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:27.100
again star. It has evolved into a wolf ray at type star. Astronomers will get to watch

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:32.940
its continued evolution on human time scales. While one star restarts its life, crews on

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.600
Earth prepared to restart their own journeys to orbit.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:42.060
The crew of NASA's SpaceX crew 13 mission entered quarantine late Thursday at Johnson Space

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:42.960
Center in Houston.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:48.120
Mission preparations continue ahead of an early October launch to the International Space

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:48.620
Station.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:55.280
NASA astronauts Jessica Watkins and Luke Delaney, along with CSA astronaut Joshua Kutrick,

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:56.360
are part of the crew.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:02.600
From crew preparations to a striking view of our own galaxy, a NASA astronaut captured an

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.400
image of the Milky Way from the International Space Station.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.380
The photo shows the galaxy's structure against the backdrop of Earth.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:15.160
Such images highlight the unique vantage point offered by the station, from striking images

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.320
to everyday infrastructure under pressure.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:24.140
Snow drought and unusually warm temperatures in the Colorado Basin in 2026 drove down reservoir

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.140
water storage in the U.S. West.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:29.540
Lake Powell reached record low levels as a result.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:33.320
NASA Earth Observations tracked the decline across the basin.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:37.740
The data highlight ongoing drought impacts on Western water supplies.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:43.540
From Earth's changing water cycle to community discussions on future missions, participants

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:47.920
joined the ad-astra community science workshop in Pasadena, California.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.180
The workshop brought together community members to discuss future directions.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:57.020
NASA science shared a debrief and outlined next steps following the event.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:03.520
from planning science to one final launch note. Keep an eye on the crew 13 launch window as early

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:09.460
October draws closer. That covers today's space and science news. Share this with a fellow space

